13:26:16 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I get why it feels obvious - it's a common joke and a lot of people's personal experience seems to confirm it. But "everyone knows it" and "it's actually true" aren't the same thing, and this particular stereotype has some real complications when you look closer: - **Survey data is genuinely inconsistent.** Some studies find women report cleaning more; others find no significant difference in overall tidiness, just different priorities (e.g., visible clutter vs. deep cleaning). - **Confirmation bias is powerful here.** If you already believe "guys are messy," you'll notice messy guys and mentally file away tidy ones as exceptions. - **The comparison is often unfair.** A lot of "men are messier" claims come from comparing men to their mothers or partners who did unpaid household labor for them - it's measuring a learned habit gap, not something inherent. - **Selection effects skew perception.** Messy male roommates in sitcoms and messy exes in stories get more airtime than tidy ones. That said, I'm not saying you're wrong to notice a pattern in your own life - it's possible your brother, his friends, guys you've dated, etc. really do skew messier. That's a real observation about your specific sample. I just wouldn't extend it to "males inherently are less tidy" as a biological or even reliably statistical truth. It's more socially learned and situational than that. So: is this actually about your brother, or has this been a pattern you've noticed with multiple guys in your life?
