13:27:28 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's actually a fair pushback, and I'll be straight with you: the "before anyone's taught them anything" framing is the crux, and it's harder to establish than it sounds. Here's the issue - kids are gendered from literally birth, not from some later age when they're old enough to "learn." Studies on infant treatment show adults handle, talk to, and react to babies differently based on perceived sex within days of birth, often without realizing it. By the time a kid is old enough to be observably "messy" (say, age 3-5), they've already had years of differential treatment: different toys, different praise/punishment patterns, different tolerance for roughness vs. neatness, parents' own unconscious expectations shaping how much cleanup they enforce. So "little boys before conditioning" isn't really a thing we can cleanly observe - conditioning is already baked in by toddlerhood. That said, I'll actually concede something to you: there is some evidence of average behavioral differences in early childhood - boys, on average, tend toward more physically active, exploratory play (throwing, building/destroying, running), while girls' play skews more toward fine-motor and relational activities. That could plausibly translate into more visible "mess" as a side effect of activity style, not because of some intent or trait called "messiness." That's a real, more modest, biologically-plausible piece I wouldn't wave away. So: not "you're imagining a natural difference" - there might be a kernel of something in activity/play style. But "boys are just inherently less tidy" as a personality trait, verified in early childhood free of social influence? That's a much harder claim to actually prove, because the "control group" (a kid raised with zero gendered treatment) doesn't really exist to compare against.
